1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,600
In August 1989, a spacecraft
flew past Neptune, more than

2
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:08,160
four billion kilometres from
Earth, and took pictures of it.

3
00:00:08,672 --> 00:00:12,932
Its camera had no memory card, and
no sensor like the one in your phone.

4
00:00:13,512 --> 00:00:18,272
It was built around a television
tube, designed in the 1970s.

5
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:22,430
And yet the pictures
arrived. Thousands of them.

6
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:26,710
So how do you send a photograph home
from the edge of the Solar System?

7
00:00:27,745 --> 00:00:31,065
To answer that, we have to go
back twelve years before Voyager's

8
00:00:31,295 --> 00:00:34,705
launch, to the first close-up
pictures of another planet.

9
00:00:35,457 --> 00:00:39,337
In July 1965, a small
probe called Mariner 4

10
00:00:39,567 --> 00:00:42,337
flew past Mars and took
twenty-two pictures.

11
00:00:43,062 --> 00:00:46,522
Its camera was a vidicon, a
kind of television camera tube.

12
00:00:46,842 --> 00:00:48,922
It didn't produce a photo.

13
00:00:49,382 --> 00:00:54,542
It produced a signal, rising and falling
as the picture was scanned line by line.

14
00:00:54,830 --> 00:00:59,150
But the probe's radio was far too
weak to send a live television signal.

15
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:01,270
So it did something clever.

16
00:01:01,910 --> 00:01:05,370
It chopped every picture into
two hundred by two hundred dots.

17
00:01:06,109 --> 00:01:09,869
The brightness of each dot became
a number from zero to sixty-three.

18
00:01:09,949 --> 00:01:11,569
Six bits per dot.

19
00:01:12,242 --> 00:01:14,722
The numbers went onto a
tape recorder, and then

20
00:01:14,952 --> 00:01:18,382
home by radio, at eight and
a third bits per second.

21
00:01:19,202 --> 00:01:22,182
At that speed, one picture
took about eight hours.

22
00:01:22,657 --> 00:01:24,917
And when the numbers
reached Earth, the engineers

23
00:01:25,147 --> 00:01:27,997
at JPL didn't want to
wait for the computers.

24
00:01:28,757 --> 00:01:31,957
They printed the raw numbers
on long strips of paper,

25
00:01:32,197 --> 00:01:34,777
laid them side by side,
and coloured them in by

26
00:01:35,007 --> 00:01:38,297
hand with pastels, like
a paint-by-numbers kit.

27
00:01:39,137 --> 00:01:42,717
The first close-up image of Mars
was a drawing made from data.

28
00:01:43,495 --> 00:01:45,615
And that's the chain this story is about.

29
00:01:46,055 --> 00:01:47,655
A picture becomes a signal.

30
00:01:47,735 --> 00:01:49,855
The signal becomes numbers.

31
00:01:50,155 --> 00:01:52,195
The numbers travel by radio.

32
00:01:52,515 --> 00:01:55,015
And on Earth, they become a picture again.

33
00:01:55,650 --> 00:01:59,510
So was Mariner 4 a digital
camera? Not really.

34
00:01:59,970 --> 00:02:01,770
Its camera was analogue.

35
00:02:01,950 --> 00:02:04,030
Everything after the camera was digital.

36
00:02:06,610 --> 00:02:11,190
A digital camera in the modern sense
came ten years later, and not in space.

37
00:02:12,130 --> 00:02:13,750
In a lab at Kodak.

38
00:02:14,303 --> 00:02:18,503
In December 1975, an
engineer called Steven Sasson

39
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:22,223
built a camera around a
new kind of chip, a CCD.

40
00:02:22,804 --> 00:02:25,744
A CCD isn't scanned by an electron beam.

41
00:02:26,344 --> 00:02:30,884
It's a grid of tiny light sensors,
each measuring its own patch of light.

42
00:02:31,484 --> 00:02:33,784
The grid is built into the chip itself.

43
00:02:35,092 --> 00:02:38,932
Sasson's grid was a hundred
by a hundred. Black and white.

44
00:02:39,672 --> 00:02:43,212
Recording one picture onto a cassette
tape took twenty-three seconds.

45
00:02:43,953 --> 00:02:45,713
Kodak never sold it.

46
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:48,553
But it answers a question
people often ask.

47
00:02:49,213 --> 00:02:54,713
Did we have digital cameras
in the 1970s? Yes. In a lab.

48
00:02:55,985 --> 00:02:58,505
Voyager's cameras, though,
belonged to the other branch.

49
00:02:58,585 --> 00:03:01,225
The one that started with Mariner.

50
00:03:01,785 --> 00:03:05,265
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

51
00:03:05,885 --> 00:03:07,505
It really helps the channel.

52
00:03:09,875 --> 00:03:13,675
Voyager 1 and Voyager 2
were launched in 1977.

53
00:03:14,335 --> 00:03:16,195
Each carried two cameras.

54
00:03:16,955 --> 00:03:20,855
A wide-angle one for big scenes,
and a narrow-angle one for detail.

55
00:03:21,623 --> 00:03:24,703
Both were slow-scan
vidicons, modified versions

56
00:03:24,933 --> 00:03:27,663
of the cameras that had
flown on earlier Mariners.

57
00:03:28,311 --> 00:03:30,411
Light from a planet
left a pattern of charge

58
00:03:30,641 --> 00:03:33,531
on a thin layer of selenium and sulphur.

59
00:03:34,011 --> 00:03:37,391
That layer could hold the image
for more than a minute and a half.

60
00:03:37,751 --> 00:03:39,651
Then an electron beam swept across it.

61
00:03:39,881 --> 00:03:44,251
Eight hundred lines, and eight
hundred points on each line.

62
00:03:44,868 --> 00:03:47,708
Reading out one frame
took forty-eight seconds.

63
00:03:48,568 --> 00:03:53,448
Each point became a number from zero to
two hundred and fifty-five. Eight bits.

64
00:03:54,225 --> 00:03:59,185
That's sixteen times as many dots as
Mariner 4, and four times as many shades.

65
00:03:59,625 --> 00:04:02,925
More than five million
bits in a single picture.

66
00:04:03,517 --> 00:04:05,677
By today's standards, that's tiny.

67
00:04:06,577 --> 00:04:08,637
About six hundred and
forty kilobytes of raw

68
00:04:08,867 --> 00:04:12,177
image data, less than one
photo from your phone.

69
00:04:12,877 --> 00:04:16,457
And the radio link added extra
bits to protect it from noise.

70
00:04:17,207 --> 00:04:21,827
But taking the picture was never
the hard part. Getting it home was.

71
00:04:24,117 --> 00:04:27,870
In 1979, at Jupiter, Voyager could send a

72
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:30,557
hundred and fifteen
thousand bits per second.

73
00:04:31,477 --> 00:04:33,257
One picture in under a minute.

74
00:04:33,743 --> 00:04:37,263
When Earth was out of view, or
the link was busy, pictures went

75
00:04:37,493 --> 00:04:40,923
onto a tape recorder, with room
for about a hundred of them.

76
00:04:41,476 --> 00:04:43,716
But every time the distance doubles, the

77
00:04:43,946 --> 00:04:46,476
signal reaching Earth
gets four times weaker.

78
00:04:47,183 --> 00:04:49,783
At Neptune, Voyager 2
would be about six times

79
00:04:50,013 --> 00:04:52,343
as far from Earth as
it had been at Jupiter.

80
00:04:53,063 --> 00:04:57,763
Without changes, it would manage
around three thousand bits per second.

81
00:04:58,417 --> 00:05:01,937
At that rate, a single picture
would take almost half an hour.

82
00:05:04,277 --> 00:05:06,717
So the engineers improved both ends.

83
00:05:07,677 --> 00:05:10,057
On Earth, the largest
dishes of NASA's Deep Space

84
00:05:10,287 --> 00:05:13,957
Network were rebuilt, from
sixty-four metres to seventy.

85
00:05:14,336 --> 00:05:19,036
And for the Neptune flyby, they listened
together with other radio telescopes,

86
00:05:19,276 --> 00:05:23,656
including the twenty-seven dishes of
the Very Large Array in New Mexico.

87
00:05:24,352 --> 00:05:26,072
The other end was harder.

88
00:05:26,832 --> 00:05:30,332
Voyager 2 was already beyond
Saturn, and nobody could touch it.

89
00:05:30,412 --> 00:05:32,632
So they changed its software.

90
00:05:33,831 --> 00:05:35,571
Voyager's computers were tiny.

91
00:05:36,251 --> 00:05:40,411
Its flight data computer ran about
eighty thousand instructions a second.

92
00:05:41,111 --> 00:05:42,891
Your phone manages billions.

93
00:05:43,773 --> 00:05:48,173
Engineers on Earth wrote a new program
and uploaded it to the backup computer.

94
00:05:49,093 --> 00:05:53,153
From then on, Voyager 2 didn't
send the brightness of every dot.

95
00:05:53,834 --> 00:05:57,134
It sent how much each dot
differed from the one next to it.

96
00:05:57,674 --> 00:06:00,354
In dark space and smooth
clouds, those differences

97
00:06:00,584 --> 00:06:04,254
are small numbers, and small
numbers need fewer bits.

98
00:06:04,961 --> 00:06:07,661
A typical picture shrank by sixty percent.

99
00:06:07,891 --> 00:06:10,461
And a new error-correcting code cut down

100
00:06:10,691 --> 00:06:13,061
the extra bits that
protected it from noise.

101
00:06:13,827 --> 00:06:19,187
At Neptune, in 1989, Voyager 2 sent more
than twenty thousand bits per second.

102
00:06:19,587 --> 00:06:22,287
Almost seven times what
it would have managed.

103
00:06:22,587 --> 00:06:25,207
A picture in about a minute and a half.

104
00:06:27,677 --> 00:06:31,117
So, did we have digital
cameras in the 1970s?

105
00:06:31,717 --> 00:06:33,637
In a lab at Kodak, yes.

106
00:06:34,157 --> 00:06:36,637
But the cameras that photographed Jupiter,

107
00:06:36,897 --> 00:06:40,077
Saturn, Uranus and Neptune
were television tubes.

108
00:06:40,876 --> 00:06:45,616
What made them digital was turning
that signal into numbers. The numbers.

109
00:06:45,996 --> 00:06:48,116
The tape. The radio.

110
00:06:48,196 --> 00:06:52,216
And software that could be rewritten
from billions of kilometres away.

111
00:06:55,456 --> 00:07:00,136
And the most famous picture those cameras
ever took wasn't of a planet they visited.

112
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:03,800
By 1990, Voyager 1 had passed Jupiter and

113
00:07:04,030 --> 00:07:06,960
Saturn, and was heading
out of the Solar System.

114
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:09,580
Its planetary work was done.

115
00:07:10,157 --> 00:07:13,957
Back in 1981, the astronomer
Carl Sagan, a member

116
00:07:14,187 --> 00:07:17,497
of the imaging team, had
proposed one last picture.

117
00:07:18,037 --> 00:07:20,837
Turn the cameras around,
and look back at home.

118
00:07:21,412 --> 00:07:26,132
On the 14th of February 1990,
Voyager 1 took sixty frames.

119
00:07:26,432 --> 00:07:29,652
A portrait of the Solar
System, from beyond Neptune.

120
00:07:30,491 --> 00:07:32,651
Six planets made it into the frames.

121
00:07:33,491 --> 00:07:36,991
Venus, Earth, Jupiter,
Saturn, Uranus and Neptune.

122
00:07:37,779 --> 00:07:40,799
Voyager was about six
billion kilometres away.

123
00:07:41,419 --> 00:07:44,879
From there, Earth covered about
an eighth of a single pixel.

124
00:07:45,642 --> 00:07:48,802
The streak of light around it
isn't something out in space.

125
00:07:49,302 --> 00:07:52,322
It's sunlight, scattered
inside the camera.

126
00:07:53,036 --> 00:07:55,376
Three exposures through
colour filters were combined

127
00:07:55,606 --> 00:07:58,776
into the image we now
call the Pale Blue Dot.

128
00:07:59,176 --> 00:08:02,876
Every person who has ever lived
lived somewhere inside it.

129
00:08:06,216 --> 00:08:08,956
Thirty-four minutes after the
pictures of Earth were taken,

130
00:08:09,036 --> 00:08:12,036
Voyager 1's cameras were
switched off, to save power.

131
00:08:12,716 --> 00:08:14,596
They were never turned on again.

132
00:08:15,231 --> 00:08:17,691
The pictures waited on the tape recorder.

133
00:08:18,491 --> 00:08:22,051
The last of the data reached
Earth on the 1st of May 1990.

134
00:08:22,131 --> 00:08:24,891
Almost eleven weeks later.

135
00:08:25,465 --> 00:08:28,345
A television tube. Eight bits per dot.

136
00:08:28,425 --> 00:08:30,885
A transmitter of about twenty watts.

137
00:08:31,622 --> 00:08:34,672
And a family portrait that
took eleven weeks to come home.
